course: dictionary
lesson: 15145
title: Words for power: sceptre
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: A sceptre is a decorated rod or staff that a king or queen carries at ceremonies. It is a sign of power, so when you see one in a picture, you know the person holding it is a ruler.
explain: Use the clues around the word. «The queen held her golden sceptre high as the crowd cheered.» She holds it high at a ceremony, and people cheer, so it must be something important. Try your meaning in the sentence: "The queen held her golden rod of power high" still makes sense.
explain: The word can also mean royal power itself. «He took the sceptre after his father died and ruled for forty years.» You cannot take a rod and rule for forty years, so here sceptre means the power to rule. Context helps you choose between the object and the power.
fact: A sceptre is a decorated rod or staff.
fact: Kings and queens carry sceptres at ceremonies as a sign of their power.
fact: Many sceptres have jewels on them, and some have a cross or another shape on top.
fact: In the Bible story of Queen Esther, the king holds out his gold sceptre to show she may come near.
fact: «Sceptre» can also mean royal power itself.
exercise: «The king raised his golden sceptre, and the crowd fell silent.» What is a sceptre? | A small bird that sings at dawn | A type of castle wall | A heavy crown worn on the head | *A decorated rod carried by a king or queen
exercise: In the Bible story, the king held out his gold sceptre to Queen Esther. Why did he do this? | To hide it from the guards | *To show she might come near | To ask for a drink | To give her a present for her birthday
exercise: «The sceptre passed to the new queen when her father died.» Here, what does sceptre mean? | A walking stick | A jewel for a necklace | A kind of pen | *Royal power itself
test: «The royal sceptre sparkled with jewels on top.» What sparkled? | A pair of shoes | A crown | The castle wall | *The sceptre
test: In a crown jewels display, you see an orb and a sceptre together. What is the sceptre? | *A decorated rod carried at ceremonies | A kite | A bell | A spoon
exam: In «He took the sceptre and ruled for forty years», what does sceptre mean? | *Royal power | A sleepy cat | A wooden spoon | A pen
